{
  "title": "Gateway API won't fix your Ingress mess",
  "content": "kubernetes gateway tends to expose the difference between working and being trustworthy. It's fine in staging because staging never sees the traffic pattern that breaks it. This is the writeup that includes the part that usually goes wrong.\n\nThe Kubernetes Gateway API is not a better Ingress. It is a different contract between the platform team and everyone else. Most platform teams adopting it because Ingress is \"deprecated\" or \"limited\" are about to inherit a second, parallel configuration surface with its own CRDs, its own RBAC model, and its own upgrade treadmill — while the original Ingress objects stay exactly where they are, because removing them breaks every tenant who filed a ticket last quarter. The contrarian take is simple: if your problem is that developers cannot self-serve HTTP routing safely, Gateway API is worth the migration cost. If your problem is that your Ingress manifests are messy, Gateway API will make them messier, because it gives you more knobs and more ways to misconfigure them. The API is not the fix; the ownership model it enables is. This post is about which teams actually benefit, which ones should wait, and how to tell the difference before you spend a quarter on a migration that changes nothing for your users.\n\n## The stance: Gateway API is an ownership boundary, not an Ingress upgrade\n\nThe common framing is that Ingress is old and Gateway API is new, so teams should migrate. That framing is wrong in a way that costs real money. Ingress and Gateway API solve the same layer-7 routing problem, but they encode different assumptions about who is allowed to touch what. Ingress assumes a single controller with a vendor-specific annotation soup, and in practice the platform team owns every object because the annotations are too dangerous for application teams to edit. Gateway API splits the object model into roles: a cluster-scoped `GatewayClass` owned by the infrastructure provider, a `Gateway` owned by the platform team, and `HTTPRoute` objects owned by application teams. That split is the entire value proposition. If your org does not want application teams creating routing objects, you get none of the benefit and all of the CRD surface area.\n\nSo the honest position is: adopt Gateway API when you are ready to delegate route ownership. Do not adopt it because the word \"Ingress\" feels legacy. The API will not clean up a mess that was created by unclear ownership; it will replicate that mess across more object types.\n\n## Why \"Ingress is deprecated, migrate\" is the wrong reason\n\nIngress is not deprecated. The `networking.k8s.io/v1` Ingress API is stable and has been since Kubernetes 1.19. It is frozen in the sense that the community is not adding features to it, but frozen is not removed. The Gateway API project itself describes Ingress as continuing to be supported. Anyone telling you Ingress is going away is either confusing it with the old `extensions/v1beta1` version that was removed, or repeating a vendor blog post that wants you on their newer controller.\n\nThe practical consequence: a migration driven by \"deprecation fear\" has no deadline and no forcing function. Teams start it, discover that every annotation on every Ingress maps to a different Gateway API filter or policy attachment, and stall halfway with both APIs live. That half-migrated state is worse than either endpoint, because now you have two routing planes, two sets of debugging tools, and two places a request can be misrouted.\n\nThe better reason to migrate is a capability gap you can name. Examples that are real and documented: Ingress has no first-class way to express header-based matching, traffic weighting across backends, or request mirroring without vendor annotations. Gateway API expresses these as part of the route spec. Ingress has no standard way to attach a policy (rate limiting, auth, TLS options) to a route without an annotation that only one controller understands. Gateway API has `BackendTLSPolicy`, `GRPCRoute`, and a policy attachment model that is still maturing but is at least a shared vocabulary. If you cannot name the capability you need and the Ingress workaround you are tired of, you do not have a migration reason yet.\n\n## The mental model: three objects, three owners\n\nThe thing that actually changes for platform teams is not the YAML. It is that the API forces you to write down who owns what. The model has three layers, and each maps to a team boundary:\n\n| Object | Scope | Typical owner | What it answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GatewayClass` | Cluster | Infrastructure / platform | Which controller implements this class, and with what global config |\n| `Gateway` | Namespaced (usually a platform namespace) | Platform team | Which listeners (ports, protocols, hostnames, TLS) are exposed, and which namespaces may attach routes |\n| `HTTPRoute` (and `GRPCRoute`, `TLSRoute`) | Namespaced (tenant namespace) | Application team | How a specific host/path/header maps to backends |\n\nThe `Gateway` object has an `allowedRoutes` field on each listener that controls which namespaces can attach an `HTTPRoute` to it. That single field is the delegation mechanism. If you set it to `Same` (the default), only routes in the Gateway's own namespace can attach, and you have rebuilt Ingress with extra steps. If you set it to `All` or a label selector, application teams can create routes in their own namespaces and the platform team never sees a ticket for a new path. That is the operational change. Everything else is syntax.\n\nA minimal, correct example that shows the boundary. The platform team creates the Gateway in a `platform-ingress` namespace:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Gateway\nmetadata:\n  name: shared-gateway\n  namespace: platform-ingress\nspec:\n  gatewayClassName: example-class\n  listeners:\n    - name: https\n      protocol: HTTPS\n      port: 443\n      hostname: \"*.example.com\"\n      tls:\n        mode: Terminate\n        certificateRefs:\n          - kind: Secret\n            name: wildcard-example-com\n      allowedRoutes:\n        namespaces:\n          from: Selector\n          selector:\n            matchLabels:\n              gateway-access: \"true\"\n```\n\nAn application team in a namespace labeled `gateway-access: \"true\"` then creates its own route:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: HTTPRoute\nmetadata:\n  name: checkout\n  namespace: checkout-team\nspec:\n  parentRefs:\n    - name: shared-gateway\n      namespace: platform-ingress\n  hostnames:\n    - \"checkout.example.com\"\n  rules:\n    - matches:\n        - path:\n            type: PathPrefix\n            value: /api\n      backendRefs:\n        - name: checkout-svc\n          port: 8080\n```\n\nNote what the application team did not need: the platform team's namespace, the TLS secret, the listener port, or an annotation. Note what the platform team can still control: the hostname wildcard, the TLS termination mode, and which namespaces are allowed to attach at all. That is the contract Ingress never had.\n\n## Where the old advice is still right\n\nA few pieces of conventional wisdom survive the migration intact, and platform teams should not throw them away.\n\nFirst, most teams should not run their own Gateway controller. The Gateway API is a spec; the implementation is a controller such as the ones from the major cloud load balancers, ingress-nginx's successor work, or service mesh data planes. Running the controller is the same operational burden as running an Ingress controller, with the same upgrade and CVE cadence. If you already pay a cloud provider for a managed load balancer, check whether its Gateway API implementation is generally available before you build anything.\n\nSecond, the \"one big shared Gateway\" pattern is still the right default for most clusters. The Gateway API supports many Gateways, but each listener consumes a port and a certificate, and a fleet of per-team Gateways is a fleet of load balancers. Start with one shared Gateway per environment and use `allowedRoutes` for delegation. Split only when you have a hard isolation requirement (separate TLS certs per business unit, separate IP allowlists, separate WAF policy).\n\nThird, the debugging instinct is the same. When a route does not work, the question is still \"did the controller see it, and did it accept it?\" Gateway API actually improves this: every `Gateway` and `HTTPRoute` has a `status.parents` section with `conditions` that tell you whether the route was accepted, resolved, and programmed. That is more structured than reading controller logs to find out why an annotation was ignored, but it is the same investigation.\n\n## The decision rule: delegate or wait\n\nUse this rule, in order. It is deliberately blunt because the cost of a wrong migration is a quarter of platform time.\n\n1. **Can you name the Ingress workaround you are tired of?** If the answer is \"annotations are ugly,\" wait. If the answer is \"we cannot do canary weighting without a vendor annotation that breaks on controller upgrade,\" you have a reason.\n2. **Are application teams going to own `HTTPRoute` objects?** If the platform team will write every route anyway, Gateway API adds CRDs without adding delegation. Stay on Ingress until the ownership model changes.\n3. **Does your chosen controller implement the features you need, at the version you need?** The Gateway API has graduated features (the core `Gateway`, `HTTPRoute`, `GatewayClass` are GA) and experimental ones (`BackendTLSPolicy`, some filter types, `GRPCRoute` maturity varies by controller). Check the controller's conformance report before you promise a capability to a tenant.\n4. **Do you have a rollback plan that keeps both APIs live?** During migration, a single hostname can be served by an Ingress and an `HTTPRoute` at the same time only if they point at different controllers or the same controller handles both. Plan per-hostname cutover, not big-bang.\n\nIf any of the first three answers is \"no,\" the correct decision is to wait and revisit when it changes. That is not a failure; it is avoiding a migration that would have produced a second routing plane with no user-visible benefit.\n\n## Failure modes that show up after the migration, not during it\n\nThe migration itself is mostly mechanical: translate host/path rules, replace annotations with filters or policy attachments, move TLS secrets to `certificateRefs`. The failures appear in the weeks after, when tenants start using the new delegation. Three are common enough to plan for.\n\n**Route attachment sprawl.** Once application teams can create `HTTPRoute` objects, they will, and some will attach to the shared Gateway with a hostname that collides with another team's. Gateway API does not prevent two routes from claiming the same hostname and path; the controller picks a winner based on its own conflict resolution, and the loser gets a condition on `status.parents` that nobody reads. The fix is a policy, not a feature: either assign hostname prefixes per namespace via a label convention, or run an admission policy that rejects `HTTPRoute` objects whose `hostnames` do not match the namespace's assigned domain. Plan for this before you flip `allowedRoutes` to `All`.\n\n**The `GatewayClass` becomes a shared dependency.** Every `Gateway` references a `GatewayClass`, and the `GatewayClass` carries controller-level configuration (which load balancer type, which global parameters). Changing it is a cluster-wide operation that can reprogram every listener at once. Treat `GatewayClass` changes like a cluster upgrade: staged, announced, and reversible. Teams that treat it as a per-team setting discover that one edit took down routing for every tenant.\n\n**Status conditions are the new logs.** The structured `status.parents` conditions are better than annotation guessing, but only if tenants know to look. A route that is not accepted shows `Accepted: False` with a reason such as `NotAllowedByListeners` or `NoMatchingParent`. Platform teams should document the three or four conditions that matter and put them in the onboarding doc, because the failure mode is a developer staring at a 404 with no idea that their route was never attached.\n\n## What to check before you commit\n\nBefore you write a single `HTTPRoute`, verify the controller, not the API. Gateway API conformance is reported per feature, and a controller that passes core conformance may still not implement the filter or policy you are counting on. The upstream project publishes conformance reports; read the one for your controller version, not the marketing page. Then check two things in your own cluster: whether the CRDs are installed at the version your manifests expect (`kubectl get crd gateways.gateway.networking.k8s.io -o jsonpath='{.spec.versions[*].name}'`), and whether your RBAC already allows application-team service accounts to create `httproutes` in their namespaces. If RBAC is still platform-only, you have built the object model without the delegation, and the migration will not change anything for your developers.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Is Ingress deprecated in Kubernetes?**\nNo. The `networking.k8s.io/v1` Ingress API is stable and remains supported. What happened is that the older `extensions/v1beta1` Ingress version was removed in Kubernetes 1.22, which is where a lot of the \"Ingress is deprecated\" talk originates. The Gateway API project positions itself as the successor for new feature work, but Ingress objects continue to function and controllers continue to reconcile them.\n\n**Can I run Ingress and Gateway API at the same time?**\nYes, and most migrations do. They are separate APIs reconciled by controllers, and a cluster can have an Ingress controller and a Gateway controller running side by side. The risk is not technical conflict but operational confusion: two routing planes mean two places to debug a 404. Plan a per-hostname cutover so each hostname has exactly one authoritative routing object at any time.\n\n**Do application teams need cluster-admin to create HTTPRoutes?**\nNo. `HTTPRoute` is namespaced, so a role binding in the team's namespace is enough, provided the `Gateway` listener's `allowedRoutes` permits that namespace to attach. The platform team controls attachment via the Gateway; the application team controls routing within the hosts and paths the Gateway exposes. That separation is the point of the API.\n\n**What replaces Ingress annotations like rewrite-target or rate limiting?**\nRewrites are expressed as `URLRewrite` filters on `HTTPRoute` rules. Rate limiting and auth are not core Gateway API fields; they are attached through the policy attachment model, and support depends on the controller. This is the area where \"Gateway API is portable\" is weakest in practice: the core routing spec is portable, but policy attachments are still controller-specific. Check your controller's documentation before assuming a policy you rely on today has a direct equivalent.\n\n## The 30-minute check\n\nOpen your current Ingress manifests and count how many rely on a controller-specific annotation for behavior you would lose on migration. Then run `kubectl get gatewayclass` in a cluster where your chosen controller is installed and read the conformance report for that controller version. If the annotation count is low and the conformance report covers your features, you have a migration case; if the annotation count is high and the report is thin, you have a reason to wait, and you just saved a quarter of platform time by finding out in half an hour.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Kubernetes documentation](https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
